WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
David compares annuity income options using the same amount of money.

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.720
Pure life, also called straight life or life only, pays for

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.980
his covered lifetime and stops at his death, without an added

4
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.080
refund or guaranteed payment period.

5
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.340
If he dies soon after income begins, a beneficiary does not

6
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.960
automatically receive the amount he originally paid.

7
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.340
If he lives much longer, lifetime payments continue under the promise.

8
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.620
Compared with otherwise similar options that add survivor or refund

9
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:34.460
guarantees, pure life generally provides a larger initial periodic payment.

10
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.900
That comparison assumes the same funding and comparable contract terms.

11
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.060
A larger check comes with a different protection pattern.

12
00:00:42.661 --> 00:00:48.041
Now assume David chooses life income with ten years certain.

13
00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:53.241
If he dies after exactly four years of payments, the remaining

14
00:00:53.241 --> 00:00:58.221
six years of guaranteed payments continue to the designated recipient under

15
00:00:58.221 --> 00:01:02.961
the option. If he is still living after ten years, his

16
00:01:02.961 --> 00:01:07.741
life payments continue. The ten-year period is a minimum payment guarantee,

17
00:01:08.421 --> 00:01:10.161
not a limit on his life income.

18
00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:14.401
Compare that with a ten-year period-certain-only option.

19
00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:19.021
That option pays for the stated ten years and ends even

20
00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:20.341
if David remains alive.

21
00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:24.041
The words life with change the result.

22
00:01:25.341 --> 00:01:29.121
Read whether the promise lasts for a lifetime, a defined period,

23
00:01:29.721 --> 00:01:31.141
or the longer of those two.

24
00:01:31.781 --> 00:01:37.041
A life-with-refund option pays lifetime income and adds a stated

25
00:01:37.041 --> 00:01:41.321
minimum-total protection if the annuitant dies early.

26
00:01:42.421 --> 00:01:46.821
Assume one hundred thousand dollars is the contract's refundable purchase

27
00:01:46.821 --> 00:01:51.341
amount and thirty thousand dollars has been paid when David dies.

28
00:01:52.261 --> 00:01:56.421
With no other adjustment, seventy thousand dollars remains under that refund

29
00:01:56.421 --> 00:02:01.121
promise. A cash-refund option pays that amount in one sum.

30
00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:06.361
An installment-refund option continues the stated installments until the

31
00:02:06.361 --> 00:02:08.241
required total has been paid.

32
00:02:09.021 --> 00:02:13.181
Those beneficiary installments do not automatically last for the

33
00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:18.421
beneficiary's lifetime. If David instead lives long enough to

34
00:02:18.421 --> 00:02:23.401
receive more than the purchase amount, his life income still continues

35
00:02:23.401 --> 00:02:28.257
under the contract. Rosa and David want income to continue

36
00:02:28.517 --> 00:02:29.817
while either of them lives.

37
00:02:30.697 --> 00:02:33.877
A joint-and-survivor option can provide that protection.

38
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:38.397
Suppose the stated payment is two thousand dollars each month while

39
00:02:38.397 --> 00:02:42.837
both live, with seventy-five percent continuing to the survivor.

40
00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:47.537
After the triggering death, the survivor receives fifteen hundred dollars

41
00:02:47.537 --> 00:02:49.677
monthly under those assumptions.

42
00:02:49.677 --> 00:02:54.417
A full-survivor option would continue the stated full amount;

43
00:02:55.057 --> 00:02:57.037
other percentages produce different results.

44
00:02:58.117 --> 00:03:01.937
The words joint or two lives alone do not establish the

45
00:03:01.937 --> 00:03:06.197
continuation amount. Read whose death triggers a reduction and whether

46
00:03:06.197 --> 00:03:08.477
payments continue to the last survivor.

47
00:03:09.357 --> 00:03:13.597
That is different from an arrangement expressly payable only while both

48
00:03:13.597 --> 00:03:18.628
people remain alive. A fixed-period option sets the

49
00:03:18.628 --> 00:03:22.108
number of payment periods, and the available funds and stated interest

50
00:03:22.108 --> 00:03:24.648
assumptions determine the periodic amount.

51
00:03:25.208 --> 00:03:29.328
A fixed-amount option instead sets the payment size, with the available

52
00:03:29.328 --> 00:03:32.868
funds and interest determining how long payments can last.

53
00:03:33.768 --> 00:03:38.495
In a simplified zero-interest example, sixty thousand dollars paid at one

54
00:03:38.495 --> 00:03:41.348
thousand dollars per month lasts sixty months.

55
00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:46.168
Changing the amount to fifteen hundred dollars shortens that to forty

56
00:03:46.168 --> 00:03:51.388
months. Those are teaching calculations, not lifetime guarantees or insurer

57
00:03:51.388 --> 00:03:55.808
quotes. A client who needs lifelong income must not mistake a

58
00:03:55.808 --> 00:03:58.848
chosen monthly withdrawal amount for a promise that money can never

59
00:03:58.848 --> 00:04:03.402
run out. Rosa asks whether the highest available initial payment is

60
00:04:03.402 --> 00:04:04.422
always the best choice.

61
00:04:05.222 --> 00:04:09.102
It is not. Compare the need for income during one or

62
00:04:09.102 --> 00:04:14.002
two lifetimes, a minimum amount for beneficiaries, available cash elsewhere

63
00:04:14.962 --> 00:04:16.382
and the effect of inflation.

64
00:04:17.422 --> 00:04:21.402
A fixed dollar payment can buy less as prices rise, even

65
00:04:21.402 --> 00:04:22.902
when every promised payment arrives.

66
00:04:23.922 --> 00:04:27.822
The choice also depends on the specific contract, because income elections

67
00:04:27.822 --> 00:04:30.422
and later access may be restricted.

68
00:04:30.422 --> 00:04:35.362
For an exam, identify what is guaranteed first: a life,

69
00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:41.502
a number of years, a minimum total or a payment size.

70
00:04:42.842 --> 00:04:47.662
Then follow the stated death or survival event to determine which

71
00:04:47.662 --> 00:04:50.862
payments stop, continue or change.
